<!-- Regression probe: an unprefixed attached property in a document that also binds a `using:`
     prefix. This combination is ordinary, compile-clean XAML, and it used to fail to render.

     Per the XAML rules `AnimatedIcon.State` carries no prefix and so belongs to the default xmlns -
     the framework's AnimatedIcon. Two things went wrong once the design host combined this page
     with the ~3MB of injected default theme resources, and BOTH are invisible in any single input
     document, which is why they took so long to find:

       1. The theme markup has unprefixed attached properties of its own. Standing alone it binds no
          CLR prefix, so they resolved correctly; injected under this page they INHERITED the
          xmlns:local below and started resolving against MicrosoftWinUISample.Controls.
       2. The injected elements re-declare their own xmlns:local="using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Controls",
          so one prefix meant two different namespaces in one document. The parser resolved a
          property element's owner through the outer binding and reported
          "The attachable property 'FallbackIconSource' was not found in type 'AnimatedIcon'" -
          naming a real, ordinary property of a real type as a missing attached one.

     Both are repaired on the COMBINED document (see FrameworkDefaultResources.RepairCombinedDocument):
     unprefixed attached properties are qualified against the default xmlns, and ambiguous prefixes
     are renamed. Keep the xmlns:local below even though nothing here uses it - it is the trigger,
     and without it this page exercises nothing that MainPage.xaml does not already cover. -->
<Page x:Class="MicrosoftWinUISample.AttachedPropertyPage"
      xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
      xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
      xmlns:local="using:MicrosoftWinUISample.Controls">
  <Grid>
    <StackPanel x:Name="RootStack" Margin="24" Spacing="12">
      <TextBlock x:Name="TitleText" Text="Unprefixed attached property" />
      <Grid x:Name="StateHost" AnimatedIcon.State="Normal">
        <TextBlock x:Name="StateText" Text="AnimatedIcon.State is set on my parent Grid" />
      </Grid>
    </StackPanel>
  </Grid>
</Page>
